{% import "_macros.j2" as m %}
{# The labeled set — one template for what used to be three ids (`concept-breakdown`, `card-row`,
   `icon-list`). They were never three *shapes*: all three are "N parallel labeled concepts", and
   the catalog picked between them by count and body length. Three Match rules for one shape made
   this the family the fill misclassified most, and a wrong pick cost real things (a set demoted to
   the row form loses nothing visually, but one demoted the other way loses its per-concept icon or
   its prose room).
   So the *shape* is the classification and the arrangement is a field: `format` picks between the
   three presentations — `grid` (cards, the default), `editorial` (flat) and `row`. All three are
   **grids**: a labeled set is N *parallel* concepts, and parallel concepts read side by side. A
   fourth format, `list`, stacked them one under the other in a single column, which spent the whole
   slide width on one item and made a set of peers read as a sequence — it is gone, and a set whose
   per-item prose genuinely needs a full-width column is not this shape at all (→ `content-text`, or
   split the slide). #}
{% set fmt = s.format or 'grid' %}
{% set items = s.cards or [] %}
{% call m.stage(s) %}
{% if fmt == 'editorial' %}
{# The same labeled set with the card chrome removed — a flat composition on the canvas, for the
   sets where the panel carried no meaning and only cost room. Markup is deliberately the plain
   icon + label + body triple: everything that makes it a *composition* (how many columns, where a
   partial last row sits, how the type steps down when it gets tight) is count-driven CSS. #}
<div class="editgrid">
{% for c in items %}
<div class="eitem{{ m.frag(s) }}">{{ icon(c.icon) }}<h3>{{ c.label | nocolon }}</h3>{% if c.body %}<p>{{ c.body }}</p>{% endif %}</div>
{% endfor %}
</div>
{% elif fmt == 'row' %}
<div class="cardrow">
{% for c in items %}
<div class="rcard{{ m.frag(s) }}">{{ chip(c.icon) }}<h3>{{ c.label | nocolon }}</h3><p>{{ c.body }}</p></div>
{% endfor %}
</div>
{% else %}
{# `grid` is also where an unrecognized `format` lands. An item whose `body` is empty (a bare
   anaphora) becomes a label-only card rather than a card with an empty paragraph in it. #}
<div class="cards">
{% for c in items %}
<div class="ccard{{ m.frag(s) }}">{{ icon(c.icon) }}<h3>{{ c.label | nocolon }}</h3>{% if c.body %}<p>{{ c.body }}</p>{% endif %}</div>
{% endfor %}
</div>
{% endif %}
{% endcall %}
